{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target's central argument rests on a false dichotomy: it assumes that the only possible justification for preferring a corroborated theory is an inductive belief that 'the future will resemble the past.' This ignores Popper's own explicit non-inductive justification based on verisimilitude (truth-likeness) and critical rationality. Popper argues we prefer better-corroborated theories not because they are probabilistically more likely to be true (induction), but because they have withstood our most severe attempts to falsify them, making them the best candidates for containing truth content among available options. The target claims this preference 'logically presupposes' induction, but fails to engage with or refute Popper's specific distinction between 'probability' (which he rejects) and 'corroboration as a report of past performance used for critical selection' (which he accepts). By conflating any preference for the tested with inductive prediction, the target attacks a straw man rather than Popper's actual position, rendering its conclusion that the system is 'inconsistent' unsupported.","premise":"Popper provides a non-inductive justification for preferring corroborated theories based on verisimilitude and critical selection, not on the assumption that the future must resemble the past.","successor_question":"If the preference for corroborated theories is based on their status as the best-tested survivors rather than an inductive probability assignment, does the charge of smuggling in induction still hold?","target":"5debf7b4d53013ec19c467ebcc224f05caf30aaba01604db9920b0a1dad2e07f"}]}